  "sleepRitualTitle": "Evening Ritual",
  "sleepRitualIntro": "Good sleep isn't luck, it's the result of good habits. Here are the levers that actually matter — check off the ones you put in place.",
  "sleepRitualLeversHeading": "The 6 levers of your sleep",
  "sleepRitualLeversSubtitle": "Tap a lever to see the actions and check off the ones you're putting in place.",

  "sleepPillarLightTitle": "Light",
  "sleepPillarLightIntro": "Light is the main regulator of your biological clock. Managed well, it naturally sets your sleep schedule.",
  "sleepActionLightWakeTitle": "Get daylight as soon as you wake up",
  "sleepActionLightWakeWhy": "10 to 30 minutes of natural light in the morning sets your internal clock and triggers, 14 to 16 h later, the evening release of melatonin. It's the single most powerful move for good sleep — and it happens in the morning.",
  "sleepActionLightDimTitle": "Dim the lights 1 to 2 h before bed",
  "sleepActionLightDimWhy": "Bright light in the evening tricks your brain into thinking it's still daytime and blocks melatonin. Switch to dim, warm, indirect lighting.",
  "sleepActionLightScreensTitle": "Cut screens or filter blue light",
  "sleepActionLightScreensWhy": "Blue light from screens suppresses melatonin the most. Night mode, blue-light-blocking glasses, or better yet: put the screen down.",
  "sleepActionLightDarkTitle": "Sleep in total darkness",
  "sleepActionLightDarkWhy": "Even a small light source, like a night-light or a charger LED, perceived through closed eyelids, reduces deep-sleep quality. Blackout curtains or a sleep mask.",

  "sleepPillarTempTitle": "Temperature",
  "sleepPillarTempIntro": "Falling asleep requires your body temperature to drop by about 1 °C. Anything that helps this cooling helps you sleep.",
  "sleepActionTempRoomTitle": "Keep your room around 18 °C",
  "sleepActionTempRoomWhy": "A cool room makes it easier for your body temperature to drop, which is needed to fall asleep. Too warm, and it's one of the most common causes of nighttime waking.",
  "sleepActionTempShowerTitle": "Take a warm shower 1 to 2 h before bed",
  "sleepActionTempShowerWhy": "Paradoxically, a warm shower dilates blood vessels and helps your body release heat afterward: your temperature drops faster, and sleep follows.",
  "sleepActionTempExtremitiesTitle": "Keep your extremities warm",
  "sleepActionTempExtremitiesWhy": "Cold feet constrict blood vessels and prevent your body from releasing core heat. Socks can, counterintuitively, help you fall asleep faster.",

  "sleepPillarFoodTitle": "Stimulants & food",
  "sleepPillarFoodIntro": "What you consume in the second half of the day weighs heavily on your night.",
  "sleepActionFoodCaffeineTitle": "Last caffeine 6 to 8 h before bed",
  "sleepActionFoodCaffeineWhy": "Caffeine blocks adenosine, the molecule that makes you sleepy, for 6 h or more. A mid-afternoon coffee cuts into deep sleep even without stopping you from falling asleep. Also watch tea, mate, and dark chocolate.",
  "sleepActionFoodDinnerTitle": "Eat a light dinner, early, 3 h before bed",
  "sleepActionFoodDinnerWhy": "Ongoing digestion raises body temperature and keeps the body busy, the opposite of what sleep needs. A light, early dinner clearly improves the depth of your night.",
  "sleepActionFoodLiquidsTitle": "Ease up on liquids in the evening",
  "sleepActionFoodLiquidsWhy": "Drinking too much right before bed multiplies bathroom trips at night, which fragment your sleep cycles. Hydrate mostly during the day.",
  "sleepActionFoodChoicesTitle": "Favor sleep-friendly foods",
  "sleepActionFoodChoicesWhy": "Some whole foods provide tryptophan, magnesium and glycine, precursors of melatonin and serotonin: almonds, walnuts, banana, oats, kiwi, oily fish.",

  "sleepPillarMentalTitle": "Mind & stress",
  "sleepPillarMentalIntro": "A restless mind is cause No.1 of trouble falling asleep. Calming it is a practice.",
  "sleepActionMentalDumpTitle": "Do a mental brain dump",
  "sleepActionMentalDumpWhy": "Write down what's on your mind and tomorrow's tasks. Getting thoughts out of your head and onto paper reduces the rumination that loops at bedtime.",
  "sleepActionMentalCoherenceTitle": "Practice a few minutes of coherent breathing",
  "sleepActionMentalCoherenceWhy": "Slowing your breath activates the parasympathetic system, the one in charge of rest. A few slow breathing cycles physiologically prepare the body for sleep.",
  "sleepActionMentalGratitudeTitle": "End with three gratitudes",
  "sleepActionMentalGratitudeWhy": "Thinking back on three positive moments from the day steers the mind toward calm rather than anxiety, and eases a peaceful drift to sleep.",
  "sleepActionMentalAvoidTitle": "Avoid anxiety-inducing content in the evening",
  "sleepActionMentalAvoidWhy": "News, work emails, and online debates activate your vigilance system right before sleep. Save the evening for what soothes you.",

  "sleepPillarRhythmTitle": "Rhythm & regularity",
  "sleepPillarRhythmIntro": "Sleep loves regularity above all else. A steady rhythm beats a long catch-up lie-in.",
  "sleepActionRhythmScheduleTitle": "Go to bed and wake up at consistent times",
  "sleepActionRhythmScheduleWhy": "Consistent hours, even on weekends, reinforce your biological clock. Regularity, more than duration alone, determines sleep quality.",
  "sleepActionRhythmCyclesTitle": "Respect your 90-minute cycles",
  "sleepActionRhythmCyclesWhy": "Sleep unfolds in cycles of about 90 min. Waking at the end of a cycle, rather than in deep sleep, makes waking up far easier.",
  "sleepActionRhythmSignsTitle": "Go to bed at the first signs",
  "sleepActionRhythmSignsWhy": "Yawning, heavy eyelids, itchy eyes: that's your sleep train passing by. Miss it, and you wait for the next cycle 90 min later.",
  "sleepActionRhythmNapsTitle": "Manage your naps",
  "sleepActionRhythmNapsWhy": "A 10 to 20 min nap in early afternoon recovers energy without cutting into your night. Too long or too late, and it sabotages your evening sleep onset.",

  "sleepPillarEnvTitle": "Environment",
  "sleepPillarEnvIntro": "Your bedroom should become a sanctuary your brain associates only with rest.",
  "sleepActionEnvBedTitle": "Reserve the bed for sleep",
  "sleepActionEnvBedWhy": "Working, eating or scrolling in bed blurs the mental association bed = sleep. Your brain needs to learn that getting into bed means sleeping.",
  "sleepActionEnvNoiseTitle": "Eliminate noise",
  "sleepActionEnvNoiseWhy": "Even without waking you, noise disrupts sleep depth. Earplugs or steady white noise can mask unpredictable disturbances.",
  "sleepActionEnvBeddingTitle": "Take care of your bedding",
  "sleepActionEnvBeddingWhy": "A suitable mattress and pillow prevent micro-awakenings caused by discomfort. You spend a third of your life in it: it's a health investment.",

